{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1000", "sample_idx": 30, "model_name": "mistralai/mistral-small-4-119b-2603", "content": "**Raymond III Count of Tripoli**\n\nRaymond III was Count of Tripoli from 1152 to 1187. He was the son of Raymond II of Tripoli and Hodierna of Rethel. He became count at a young age when his father was assassinated in 1152. During his early years as count his mother Hodierna ruled in his name while he was educated in the court of his maternal uncle King Baldwin III of Jerusalem. He was knighted in 1161 and took full control of the county in 1164. Raymond III played a significant role in the politics of the Crusader states during the 12th century.\n\n**highlighted section part 1**\n\nRaymond III first appears in historical records during the reign of his father. He was born sometime before 1140 and was the only son of Raymond II and Hodierna. His father was assassinated in 1152 by a group of Assassins which left the county in a precarious position. Hodierna ruled as regent for her young son while he was sent to Jerusalem to be educated under the guardianship of his uncle King Baldwin III. He was knighted in 1161 and began taking more active roles in the governance of Tripoli. By 1164 he had reached adulthood and assumed full control of the county.\n\n**highlighted section part 2**\n\nAs Count of Tripoli Raymond III was involved in the broader politics of the Crusader states. He was present at the Battle of Harim in 1164 where he was captured by Nur ad-Din. He was held prisoner for nearly ten years until a ransom was paid for his release in 1173. Upon his return he found his county in a weakened state. Raymond III worked to restore stability and strengthen Tripoli’s defenses. He also played a key role in the succession dispute following the death of King Amalric of Jerusalem in 1174. He initially supported Miles of Plancy as regent but later backed Raymond of Antioch when Miles was assassinated.\n\n**highlighted section part 3**\n\nRaymond III is best known for his involvement in the politics surrounding the succession of Baldwin IV of Jerusalem. He was a leading figure among the barons who opposed Guy of Lusignan’s claim to the throne. He supported Raymond of Antioch and later his namesake Raymond of Tripoli who was the count of Tripoli and a cousin of Baldwin IV. He played a crucial role in the politics leading up to the Battle of Hattin in 1187. He died shortly after the battle having played a significant role in the politics of the Crusader states for over three decades. His death marked the end of an era for the County of Tripoli which would soon face further challenges in the wake of Saladin’s victories.", "logprobs": [{"token": "**", "logprob": -0.22343038022518158}, {"token": "Ray", "logprob": -0.40838614106178284}, {"token": "mond", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " III", "logprob": -0.00010394509445177391}, {"token": " Count", "logprob": -0.011593468487262726}, {"token": " of", "logprob": -7.712543447269127e-05}, {"token": " Tripoli", "logprob": -1.2993727978027891e-05}, {"token": "**\n\n", "logprob": -0.4307023584842682}, {"token": "Ray", "logprob": -0.05565937981009483}, {"token": "mond", "logprob": -1.9073468138230965e-06}, {"token": " III", "logprob": -0.00033945043105632067}, {"token": " was", "logprob": -0.20022475719451904}, {"token": " Count", "logprob": -0.17436879873275757}, {"token": " of", "logprob": -5.960462772236497e-07}, {"token": " Tripoli", "logprob": -1.0132738680113107e-05}, {"token": " from", "logprob": -0.0008131535141728818}, {"token": " ", "logprob": -0.00045658653834834695}, {"token": "1", "logprob": -1.0847986231965479e-05}, {"token": "1", "logprob": -0.00016723664884921163}, {"token": "5", "logprob": -0.0032626751344650984}, {"token": "2", "logprob": -0.00017188502533826977}, {"token": " to", "logprob": -0.42877838015556335}, {"token": " ", "logprob": -0.004097993951290846}, {"token": "1", "logprob": -1.823885577323381e-05}, {"token": "1", "logprob": -3.576214658096433e-05